          "heading": "Lesson 1.3: Conservation and mixing calculations",
          "content": "### Mechanism and why it matters\n\nA two-endmember mass balance weights material fluxes rather than concentration contrasts, provided the elemental tracer is conserved. Identify the named chemical phase and its exchange boundaries. Distinguish measured species totals from inferred reactions; one pattern can have several mechanisms.\n\n### Sampling and measurement protocol\n\nMeasure or invent mass flow rates for each source and its component concentration; state whether a phase precipitates during mixing. Record phase, sampling support, date, concentration or ratio units, calibration and detection limit. Define which quantities are observed and which depend on an assumed model.\n\n### Competing explanations and identifiability\n\nA non-conservative species can mimic a third endmember because of sorption, biological uptake or degassing. Specify an independently testable observation capable of distinguishing this explanation from an alternative source, reaction or laboratory bias.\n\n### Worked numerical example \u2014 Two-source balance\n\n**Problem.** Mix 30 kg with 50 mg/kg and 70 kg with 150 mg/kg; calculate product concentration.\n\n**Mathematical setup:** `(30*50+70*150)/100`. After checking the listed mass, time and amount units, the calculated answer is **120 mg/kg**. Write down the inputs separately from the chosen constant or reference so an independent reviewer can recompute the result. If the expression uses a logarithm or a ratio, confirm that its argument is dimensionless; if a molar amount is involved, convert grams to moles before applying reaction coefficients. Because the values are invented for this lesson, the arithmetic demonstrates the physical scale and not a measurement-derived regional conclusion.\n\n**Sensitivity check.** Compare the sign and magnitude with a limiting case and perturb the main input by ten percent. Report whether the relationship is linear or nonlinear, and avoid overstating numerical precision.\n\n### Targeted investigation and practical interpretation\n\nCalculate the mixed concentration and write a residual term. Create a six-row synthetic table with inputs, predicted outputs and one replicate. Compare a rival process and document a falsifiable difference.\n\n### Advanced reasoning: Defining the denominator\n\nWhen a sample reports a concentration, identify exactly what mass belongs in the denominator. Dry-weight and wet-weight data cannot be compared naively; trace inventories require a material mass. A concentration ratio can be dimensionless without having any direct biological or economic interpretation. Repeat the calculation with a different reservoir mass to determine what changed physically.\n\n### Knowledge check and research questions\n\n\n1. Recalculate the numerical example using consistent units.\n2. Which competing physical process could reproduce the observation?\n3. Which new measurement would discriminate the mechanisms?\n4. Which assumed parameter creates the greatest model sensitivity?\n\n**Instructor benchmark.** 120 mg/kg, conditional on the stated model and units.",
