          "secNumber": "1.8",
          "heading": "Integrated investigation 1: Elemental inventories and geochemical reasoning capstone",
          "content": "('Reservoir accounting', 'Construct a mass-flow diagram with weathering inputs, river export, dissolved storage and atmospheric contribution. Invent five endmember concentrations between 0 and 200 mg/kg and ensure at least one flux is unmeasured. Solve the implied residual as a storage change, then show that the same concentration pattern is consistent with a different water volume. Discuss sampling cadence, detection limits and why the uncertainty in discharge may dominate the uncertainty in analytical concentration.')\n\n### Design and controls\n\nBefore computing any numbers, list the intended mechanistic question, the sampling unit, plausible confounding influences and the criteria for rejecting a proposed explanation. Separate control treatments from replicates. Specify one independent check that was not used to choose model coefficients, since a model cannot be validated merely by predicting its own calibration inputs. Synthetic scenarios are safe for classroom inference but do not replace field permissions, contamination control or specialist laboratory work.\n\n### A complete synthetic data record\n\nUse a header with the following fields: sample_id, phase, dry_mass_g or volume_L, chemical_species, measured_value, analytical_unit, uncertainty, model_assumption, replicate_id and observation_status. Every derived column should include a formula and an audit trail. Avoid blanks that could ambiguously mean zero; label missing or below-quantification values explicitly. The class should archive its original input table and a derived working table so updates to calculations cannot overwrite evidence.\n\n### Expected result and alternatives\n\nAnalyze the invented measurements with the simplest mass balance or equilibrium relationship appropriate to the question. Choose plausible parameter ranges before looking at the pattern. Next deliberately generate an alternative dataset under a different process that yields a similar headline gradient. Explain which additional variable, sampling depth, time series or reference material would distinguish them. This step addresses the practical reality that geological and chemical processes are often nonunique when seen through sparse observations.\n\n### Sensitivity and uncertainty\n\nBuild a one-at-a-time sensitivity table varying at least two inputs by 5%, 10% and 20%. Use a separate scenario for systematic calibration shift, because correlated bias does not vanish by averaging independent samples. Include sampling uncertainty when a large reservoir is represented by a few rock chips or bottles. Report the resulting range as a model-conditioned bound rather than an objectively measured confidence interval unless a statistical sampling model was justified.\n\n### Interpretation and reporting\n\nThe final deliverable is a short field-style memo with a figure, a unit-checked table, a reproducible calculation and two rival explanations. Distinguish measured variables from estimated parameters by typography or column label. Provide the domain where the simple model is defensible, including temperature, composition and phase assumptions. Avoid making health, engineering or management decisions from synthetic data; those require validated data and appropriate regulatory and professional guidance.\n\n### Further experimental design\n\nPropose two follow-up observations and rank them by discrimination power, cost and likelihood of analytical bias. A new sample that merely repeats the same confounded measurement may reduce noise without reducing the ambiguity of the hypothesis. A strategically chosen cross-phase, isotope or seasonal measurement can be more informative. Set a stopping rule: what numerical discrepancy or missing diagnostic signature would be sufficient to reject the favored model?\n\n### Research notebook prompts\n\n1. At checkpoint 1, identify which assumptions in investigation 1 are directly tested, which still require external constraints, and how one alternative reaction or transport pathway could change the conclusion without necessarily altering the measured primary tracer. Record the expected sign of the change and the observation that would distinguish it.\n2. At checkpoint 2, identify which assumptions in investigation 1 are directly tested, which still require external constraints, and how one alternative reaction or transport pathway could change the conclusion without necessarily altering the measured primary tracer. Record the expected sign of the change and the observation that would distinguish it.\n3. At checkpoint 3, identify which assumptions in investigation 1 are directly tested, which still require external constraints, and how one alternative reaction or transport pathway could change the conclusion without necessarily altering the measured primary tracer. Record the expected sign of the change and the observation that would distinguish it.\n4. At checkpoint 4, identify which assumptions in investigation 1 are directly tested, which still require external constraints, and how one alternative reaction or transport pathway could change the conclusion without necessarily altering the measured primary tracer. Record the expected sign of the change and the observation that would distinguish it.\n5. At checkpoint 5, identify which assumptions in investigation 1 are directly tested, which still require external constraints, and how one alternative reaction or transport pathway could change the conclusion without necessarily altering the measured primary tracer. Record the expected sign of the change and the observation that would distinguish it.\n6. At checkpoint 6, identify which assumptions in investigation 1 are directly tested, which still require external constraints, and how one alternative reaction or transport pathway could change the conclusion without necessarily altering the measured primary tracer. Record the expected sign of the change and the observation that would distinguish it.\n7. At checkpoint 7, identify which assumptions in investigation 1 are directly tested, which still require external constraints, and how one alternative reaction or transport pathway could change the conclusion without necessarily altering the measured primary tracer. Record the expected sign of the change and the observation that would distinguish it.\n8. At checkpoint 8, identify which assumptions in investigation 1 are directly tested, which still require external constraints, and how one alternative reaction or transport pathway could change the conclusion without necessarily altering the measured primary tracer. Record the expected sign of the change and the observation that would distinguish it.",
