          "content": "### Mechanism and why it matters\n\nThe chemical potential includes standard-state and logarithmic activity terms; equilibrium occurs when the reaction Gibbs energy is zero. Identify the named chemical phase and its exchange boundaries. Distinguish measured species totals from inferred reactions; one pattern can have several mechanisms.\n\n### Sampling and measurement protocol\n\nWrite the reaction quotient with activities and compare it with a temperature-specific equilibrium constant. Record phase, sampling support, date, concentration or ratio units, calibration and detection limit. Define which quantities are observed and which depend on an assumed model.\n\n### Competing explanations and identifiability\n\nAn equilibrium constant copied from 25 \u00b0C need not describe a deep hot aquifer. Specify an independently testable observation capable of distinguishing this explanation from an alternative source, reaction or laboratory bias.\n\n### Worked numerical example \u2014 Reaction quotient ratio\n\n**Problem.** A classroom reaction has Q=0.008 and K=0.02; calculate Q/K.\n\n**Mathematical setup:** `0.008/0.02`. After checking the listed mass, time and amount units, the calculated answer is **0.4 dimensionless**. Write down the inputs separately from the chosen constant or reference so an independent reviewer can recompute the result. If the expression uses a logarithm or a ratio, confirm that its argument is dimensionless; if a molar amount is involved, convert grams to moles before applying reaction coefficients. Because the values are invented for this lesson, the arithmetic demonstrates the physical scale and not a measurement-derived regional conclusion.\n\n**Sensitivity check.** Compare the sign and magnitude with a limiting case and perturb the main input by ten percent. Report whether the relationship is linear or nonlinear, and avoid overstating numerical precision.\n\n### Targeted investigation and practical interpretation\n\nCompute the ratio Q/K and reason about the direction of the affinity. Create a six-row synthetic table with inputs, predicted outputs and one replicate. Compare a rival process and document a falsifiable difference.\n\n### Advanced reasoning: Thermodynamic scope\n\nState the standard state and whether an input is an activity, molality or analytical total. Constants vary with temperature; ionic association and complexation change free-ion proportions. The simple classroom formula does not perform full mass-action speciation, so a physically credible field interpretation should demonstrate charge closure and consistent carbonate or redox boundaries.\n\n### Knowledge check and research questions\n\n\n1. Recalculate the numerical example using consistent units.\n2. Which competing physical process could reproduce the observation?\n3. Which new measurement would discriminate the mechanisms?\n4. Which assumed parameter creates the greatest model sensitivity?\n\n**Instructor benchmark.** 0.4 dimensionless, conditional on the stated model and units.",
