          "content": "### Mechanism and why it matters\n\nThe number of radioactive parent atoms decreases exponentially and age equations require closed-system and initial-state assumptions. Identify the named chemical phase and its exchange boundaries. Distinguish measured species totals from inferred reactions; one pattern can have several mechanisms.\n\n### Sampling and measurement protocol\n\nIdentify half-life, decay constant, daughter corrections, mineral closure and post-formation resetting. Record phase, sampling support, date, concentration or ratio units, calibration and detection limit. Define which quantities are observed and which depend on an assumed model.\n\n### Competing explanations and identifiability\n\nAn apparent isotopic age can reflect metamorphism or alteration instead of original crystallization. Specify an independently testable observation capable of distinguishing this explanation from an alternative source, reaction or laboratory bias.\n\n### Worked numerical example \u2014 Parent remaining\n\n**Problem.** A parent isotope has half-life 4.5 Ga; fraction remaining after 9 Ga.\n\n**Mathematical setup:** `2**(-9/4.5)`. After checking the listed mass, time and amount units, the calculated answer is **0.25 fraction**. Write down the inputs separately from the chosen constant or reference so an independent reviewer can recompute the result. If the expression uses a logarithm or a ratio, confirm that its argument is dimensionless; if a molar amount is involved, convert grams to moles before applying reaction coefficients. Because the values are invented for this lesson, the arithmetic demonstrates the physical scale and not a measurement-derived regional conclusion.\n\n**Sensitivity check.** Compare the sign and magnitude with a limiting case and perturb the main input by ten percent. Report whether the relationship is linear or nonlinear, and avoid overstating numerical precision.\n\n### Targeted investigation and practical interpretation\n\nFind the remaining parent fraction after an interval. Create a six-row synthetic table with inputs, predicted outputs and one replicate. Compare a rival process and document a falsifiable difference.\n\n### Advanced reasoning: Isotope normalization\n\nAlways write the isotope pair, standard and delta or epsilon scaling before manipulating numbers. An isotope ratio represents atoms of specific isotopes, not bulk atomic fractions unless transformed. Closure ages are conditional. Mass-dependent fractionation, source mixing and radiogenic ingrowth produce different patterns and may need several isotope systems to disentangle.\n\n### Knowledge check and research questions\n\n\n1. Recalculate the numerical example using consistent units.\n2. Which competing physical process could reproduce the observation?\n3. Which new measurement would discriminate the mechanisms?\n4. Which assumed parameter creates the greatest model sensitivity?\n\n**Instructor benchmark.** 0.25 fraction, conditional on the stated model and units.",
