          "heading": "Lesson 6.1: Chemical weathering indices",
          "content": "### Mechanism and why it matters\n\nSilicate weathering commonly removes mobile cations relative to less mobile Al, but compositional indices depend on mineralogy. Identify the named chemical phase and its exchange boundaries. Distinguish measured species totals from inferred reactions; one pattern can have several mechanisms.\n\n### Sampling and measurement protocol\n\nUse molar oxide proportions, correct CaO from non-silicates when possible, and report provenance caveats. Record phase, sampling support, date, concentration or ratio units, calibration and detection limit. Define which quantities are observed and which depend on an assumed model.\n\n### Competing explanations and identifiability\n\nDifferent parent rocks can yield different index values even under identical weathering intensity. Specify an independently testable observation capable of distinguishing this explanation from an alternative source, reaction or laboratory bias.\n\n### Worked numerical example \u2014 Chemical index of alteration\n\n**Problem.** Molar Al2O3=0.45, CaO*=0.10, Na2O=0.15, K2O=0.30; CIA percent.\n\n**Mathematical setup:** `100*0.45/(0.45+0.10+0.15+0.30)`. After checking the listed mass, time and amount units, the calculated answer is **45 percent**. Write down the inputs separately from the chosen constant or reference so an independent reviewer can recompute the result. If the expression uses a logarithm or a ratio, confirm that its argument is dimensionless; if a molar amount is involved, convert grams to moles before applying reaction coefficients. Because the values are invented for this lesson, the arithmetic demonstrates the physical scale and not a measurement-derived regional conclusion.\n\n**Sensitivity check.** Compare the sign and magnitude with a limiting case and perturb the main input by ten percent. Report whether the relationship is linear or nonlinear, and avoid overstating numerical precision.\n\n### Targeted investigation and practical interpretation\n\nCompute a chemical index of alteration from molar oxide inputs. Create a six-row synthetic table with inputs, predicted outputs and one replicate. Compare a rival process and document a falsifiable difference.\n\n### Advanced reasoning: Time and transport\n\nFlux follows the product of concentration and carrier transport only when measurements represent the same interval. Inventories describe storage, not instantaneous flow. For annual estimates, storm-driven changes and dry-season gaps should be treated as systematic sources of uncertainty rather than hidden by extra decimal places.\n\n### Knowledge check and research questions\n\n\n1. Recalculate the numerical example using consistent units.\n2. Which competing physical process could reproduce the observation?\n3. Which new measurement would discriminate the mechanisms?\n4. Which assumed parameter creates the greatest model sensitivity?\n\n**Instructor benchmark.** 45 percent, conditional on the stated model and units.",
