          "content": "### Mechanism and why it matters\n\nMicrobial activity and vegetation influence element cycling through uptake, remineralization and redox transformations. Identify the named chemical phase and its exchange boundaries. Distinguish measured species totals from inferred reactions; one pattern can have several mechanisms.\n\n### Sampling and measurement protocol\n\nMeasure biomass production and elemental content, with a separate pathway for leaching and gaseous loss. Record phase, sampling support, date, concentration or ratio units, calibration and detection limit. Define which quantities are observed and which depend on an assumed model.\n\n### Competing explanations and identifiability\n\nA nutrient inventory change could arise from external deposition rather than only biological uptake. Specify an independently testable observation capable of distinguishing this explanation from an alternative source, reaction or laboratory bias.\n\n### Worked numerical example \u2014 Biological elemental uptake\n\n**Problem.** 20 kg dry plant matter with 1.2 wt% phosphorus; calculate phosphorus mass in kg.\n\n**Mathematical setup:** `20*1.2/100`. After checking the listed mass, time and amount units, the calculated answer is **0.24 kg**. Write down the inputs separately from the chosen constant or reference so an independent reviewer can recompute the result. If the expression uses a logarithm or a ratio, confirm that its argument is dimensionless; if a molar amount is involved, convert grams to moles before applying reaction coefficients. Because the values are invented for this lesson, the arithmetic demonstrates the physical scale and not a measurement-derived regional conclusion.\n\n**Sensitivity check.** Compare the sign and magnitude with a limiting case and perturb the main input by ten percent. Report whether the relationship is linear or nonlinear, and avoid overstating numerical precision.\n\n### Targeted investigation and practical interpretation\n\nCalculate nutrient uptake from mass and tissue concentration. Create a six-row synthetic table with inputs, predicted outputs and one replicate. Compare a rival process and document a falsifiable difference.\n\n### Advanced reasoning: Time and transport\n\nFlux follows the product of concentration and carrier transport only when measurements represent the same interval. Inventories describe storage, not instantaneous flow. For annual estimates, storm-driven changes and dry-season gaps should be treated as systematic sources of uncertainty rather than hidden by extra decimal places.\n\n### Knowledge check and research questions\n\n\n1. Recalculate the numerical example using consistent units.\n2. Which competing physical process could reproduce the observation?\n3. Which new measurement would discriminate the mechanisms?\n4. Which assumed parameter creates the greatest model sensitivity?\n\n**Instructor benchmark.** 0.24 kg, conditional on the stated model and units.",
